# /etc/cron.d/proxmod-cron — the convergence anchor for proxmod-cron.
#
# This is the only file in this package an administrator is meant to edit, and
# it is a dpkg conffile, so your changes survive upgrades.
#
# It is NOT where cron jobs go. proxmod-cron-sync reads the definition stores
#
#     /etc/pve/proxmod/cron.cfg   (cluster-wide, replicated by pmxcfs)
#     /etc/proxmod/cron.cfg       (this node only)
#
# and rewrites /etc/cron.d/proxmod-cron-cluster and /etc/cron.d/proxmod-cron-node
# whenever their rendered content changes — which is what makes a job created on
# one node appear on the others, with no daemon and no maintainer script. Those
# two files are generated: edit them and the next sync overwrites you. Edit the
# stores through the web interface, the REST API, or `proxmod-cronctl`.
#
# Running this every minute costs a fraction of a second and no write when
# nothing changed. Lengthen the interval if you want to; the only effect is how
# long a cluster job takes to appear on the other nodes.
#
# MAILTO is deliberately not set here. The generated files empty it, because
# proxmod-cron-exec captures job output into the journal instead — but a failure
# of the sync itself has no such capture, so it should reach root the ordinary
# way.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

* * * * *	root	[ -x /usr/lib/proxmod-cron/proxmod-cron-sync ] && /usr/lib/proxmod-cron/proxmod-cron-sync
